      "heading": "Residuals and layer norm are not decoration",
      "narration": "These two wrappers look like housekeeping in the diagram and they are the reason the architecture can be a hundred layers deep, so let us take them seriously. The residual connection is written x plus Sublayer of x. You compute the sublayer as normal, and then you add the original input back on. Two things follow from that plus sign, and both are important. The first is about gradients. When backpropagation comes through an addition, the gradient splits and flows down both branches unchanged, because the derivative of x with respect to x is one. So there is a path from the loss all the way back to layer one that consists entirely of additions, and multiplying by one, however many times, is still one. Compare that with the vanishing-gradient story from Part Three: without residuals, the gradient reaching the early layers of a deep stack has been multiplied by dozens of Jacobians and is essentially noise. With them, every layer has a direct line to the loss. This is the single trick that made networks of a hundred layers trainable, it came from ResNet in computer vision, and the transformer inherited it. The second consequence is about what a block has to learn, and it is subtler and just as useful. Without a residual, a block that wants to leave its input mostly alone must learn to reproduce that input exactly through a matrix multiply and a non-linearity — which is hard, and wasteful. With a residual, doing nothing is the default: if the sublayer outputs zeros, the block is the identity. So each block only has to learn the difference it wants to make, the update rather than the whole representation. That is why you can stack ninety-six of them and the ones that turn out to have nothing useful to contribute simply learn to contribute nearly nothing, instead of actively damaging the signal. Now layer normalization. The mechanic is simple: for each token, take its vector, subtract the mean of its features, divide by their standard deviation, and then scale and shift by two learned parameters. The point is to keep the inputs to each sublayer in a well-behaved range as the residual stream accumulates update after update — without it, activations drift in scale as you go up the stack and training becomes unstable. The word to underline is layer. This normalizes across the features of one token, independently of every other token and of the batch. Contrast batch normalization, which normalizes each feature across the examples in the batch: that makes the result depend on which other examples happen to be alongside yours, it needs different behaviour at training and inference time, and it degrades badly with small batches and variable-length sequences. All three problems are fatal for language, which is why every transformer uses LayerNorm and none use BatchNorm. And one final detail that looks cosmetic and is not: where you put the norm. The original paper did post-norm — apply the sublayer, add the residual, then normalize. It works, but it is fragile, and training it at depth requires a learning-rate warmup period to stop it diverging in the first few hundred steps. Pre-norm — normalize first, then the sublayer, then add — leaves the residual path completely clean, trains stably without the warmup, and is what essentially every modern model does. It is a one-line change in the code and it decides whether large models train reliably.",
