      "narration": "Franky built the Sunny as a stack of decks, and the detail worth noticing is not the decks — it is the corridor. There is one main passage that runs straight down through all of them, and every room opens off it: the galley, the workshop, the infirmary, the aquarium, the cabins. None of those rooms sits in the corridor. You walk past their doors. Now think about what that arrangement makes possible. If Chopper needs something from the storeroom at the bottom of the ship, it comes straight up the corridor. It does not have to be handed through the galley, and then handled by whoever is in the workshop, and then passed through the infirmary, arriving changed by each of them. It travels the whole height of the ship untouched. That is the residual stream, and it is exactly the property the plus sign gives you. Information — and, running the other way, gradient — has a clean route from one end of the model to the other, because the path does not go through the sublayers, it goes past them. A ship where every deck had to be walked through to reach the next one would be the pre-residual network, and the thing at the bottom would arrive unrecognizable. Notice the second property, because it is the one people underrate. A room can be shut. If the workshop is closed for the day, nothing about the ship breaks — the corridor still runs, and everything that was going past continues to go past. Compare a design where the corridor ran through each workshop: closing one would sever the ship in half. That is exactly the difference the residual makes to a block that has nothing useful to contribute. Without it, a bad layer actively corrupts what passes through, and adding depth can make a model worse. With it, a useless block learns to output nearly zero and simply gets walked past. Doing nothing is free, so extra depth is never actively harmful, so you can afford to build ninety-six decks and let training work out which ones matter. There is one more fitting in this picture. Along the bulkhead at every deck are gauges keeping the pressure and the flow in a sensible range, and they are there for a mundane and important reason: it means the same fittings, the same pipe diameters, the same doors work on every deck. Franky did not have to design deck forty differently from deck three. That is layer normalization. By keeping the scale of what enters each sublayer well-behaved, it lets one block design be repeated identically all the way up, instead of every layer needing its own hand-tuned settings. Corridor, rooms, gauges. A residual stream, sublayers hanging off it, and normalization keeping the whole stack in range. That is a transformer, and there is genuinely nothing else in the building.",
