      "narration": "Let us finish by walking one token all the way through, and then naming the three ways this architecture gets configured, because after this everything in the course is a variation on what you are about to hear. The journey. Your text is tokenized into integers. Each integer indexes the embedding table and comes out as a vector — that is the word-embeddings lesson, and it is still layer zero. Position information is added or, with RoPE, applied inside the attention. Now the vector enters the stack. In block one, layer norm tidies it, self-attention lets it gather from every other token in the sentence, and the result is added back into its residual stream. Then layer norm again, the feedforward processes what it now holds, and that result is added in too. That is one block, and the vector that comes out is the same shape as the one that went in — which is exactly why you can repeat the block. So it does, twelve times, or ninety-six. Deep in the stack, that vector is no longer really about the token it started as; it is a representation of that position in the context of the whole sentence. At the top, a final layer norm, and then one last linear projection from the model dimension out to the size of the vocabulary — producing one score per possible token — and a softmax turns those scores into a probability distribution. That distribution is the model's output. Every large language model you will use is that path. Now the three configurations, which differ only in which parts you keep and what mask you use. Encoder-only is BERT: no causal mask, so every token attends to every other token in both directions, and the representation of each position is informed by the entire sentence. That bidirectionality makes it excellent at understanding — classification, named entity recognition, retrieval, anything where you have the whole input in hand. It cannot generate, and the reason is worth stating precisely: if every position can see every other position, then during training a position can see the answer it is meant to predict. There is nothing to generate. Decoder-only is GPT, and it is what almost everything is today. Apply the causal mask, so position i sees only positions up to i, and train the whole thing to predict the next token. Generation is then immediate — feed what you have, take the distribution at the last position, sample, append, repeat. It sacrifices bidirectional context and, as the field discovered somewhat to its own surprise, that turned out to matter far less than being able to train on any text at all and generate. Encoder-decoder keeps both stacks, and is what the original paper described, because the original task was translation. The encoder reads the source with no mask. The decoder generates the target with a causal mask, and each of its blocks has a third sublayer between the two you know: cross-attention, where the queries come from the decoder and the keys and values come from the encoder. That is the attention from the seq2seq lesson, unchanged, and it needs no new machinery — it is the same function with different arguments, which is the tidiest thing about the whole design.",
